//? Title: Bead Sort
//? Subtitle: Sorting by gravity as conjugation of a Ferrers diagram twice, its column heights as an empirical survival function, and its true cost, with Old Faithful's waiting times and datamunge's algorithms::bead_sort
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Bead sort lets rows of beads on an abacus fall under gravity, and the rows come out sorted.
We prove why: the fall turns the Ferrers diagram of the values into its conjugate partition,
and reading that diagram's rows conjugates it a second time, which gives the parts in order. The
same argument gives an \(O(n+\max a)\) algorithm with no bead moves, which is what
`algorithms::bead_sort` implements. Applied to the 272 waiting times between Old Faithful
eruptions, the column heights of the fallen diagram turn out to be \(n\) times the empirical
survival function, and the rows are the order statistics. A timing study shows the catch:
the cost grows with the largest value, not just with the number of values.
)

\toc

> Sorting on an abacus

Bead sort (Arulanandham, Calude and Dinneen 2002) sorts non-negative integers the way an abacus
would if it were tipped on its side. Each number \(a_i\) becomes a row of \(a_i\) beads, threaded on
vertical rods \(0,1,\dots,a_i-1\). Then the beads are allowed to fall. Every rod's beads slide to
the bottom, and the rows, read from the top, now hold the numbers in ascending order. No two
numbers are ever compared.

>> Why it works: conjugate partitions

Rows of left-justified beads are a *Ferrers diagram*. If the values are read as the parts of an
integer partition \(\lambda\), the diagram's column lengths are the *conjugate* partition

$$
\lambda'_j=\#\{i:a_i>j\},\qquad j=0,1,\dots,\max_ia_i-1,
$$
\alttext(lambda prime j is the number of i with a i greater than j, for j from 0 to the maximum of a minus 1.)

because rod \(j\) carries a bead from every row longer than \(j\). Gravity leaves each rod's count
unchanged but stacks its beads at the bottom. So after the fall, the row at height \(r\) (counting
the bottom row as \(r=0\)) has a bead on rod \(j\) exactly when \(\lambda'_j>r\), and its length is

$$
\rho_r=\#\{j:\lambda'_j>r\}=\#\{j:\#\{i:a_i>j\}\ge r+1\}=\#\{j:a_{(r+1)}>j\}=a_{(r+1)},
$$
\alttext(rho r is the number of j with lambda prime j greater than r, which is the number of j for which at least r plus 1 values exceed j, which is the number of j below the r plus first largest value, which equals that value.)

where \(a_{(r+1)}\) is the \((r+1)\)-th largest value. The third step holds because at least
\(r+1\) values exceed \(j\) exactly when the \((r+1)\)-th largest does. So the bottom row is the
largest value, the next row the second largest, and so on. In the language of partitions, the fall computes \(\lambda'\) and then reads off
\((\lambda')'\), and conjugation applied twice returns the parts in non-increasing order.
\(\blacksquare\)

Both conjugations can be done without moving any beads. A histogram of the values gives
\(\lambda'_j\) by a suffix sum, \(\lambda'_j=\sum_{v>j}\#\{i:a_i=v\}\), in \(O(n+\max a)\) time. Applying
the same step to \(\lambda'\) gives the sorted values. `algorithms::bead_sort` works this way,
`algorithms::bead_columns` returns \(\lambda'\), and `algorithms::bead_sort_gravity` simulates the
fall one cell per tick for comparison.

> A small abacus

`dm::datasets::faithful()` records 272 eruptions of the Old Faithful geyser in Yellowstone,
with the waiting time in minutes since the previous eruption. For a picture we take the first
twelve waiting times rounded to the nearest 10 minutes and let the beads fall.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/bead_small.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto faithful = dm::datasets::faithful();
  const auto& waiting = faithful.get_column("waiting").values();
  std::vector<unsigned> tens;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < 12; ++i) tens.push_back(static_cast<unsigned>(std::lround(waiting[i] / 10.0)));
  const auto fall = alg::bead_sort_gravity(tens);
  const auto columns = alg::bead_columns(tens);

  std::print("Values (tens of minutes): ");
  for (const unsigned v : tens) std::print("{} ", v);
  std::print("\nColumn heights λ′: ");
  for (const unsigned c : columns) std::print("{} ", c);
  auto sorted = tens;
  alg::bead_sort(sorted);
  std::print("\nSorted: ");
  for (const unsigned v : sorted) std::print("{} ", v);
  std::print("\nThe fall took {} ticks and {} single-cell bead moves; per tick: ", fall.ticks, fall.bead_moves);
  for (const long long m : fall.moves_per_tick) std::print("{} ", m);
  std::print(".\n");

  const auto draw = [](const std::vector<std::vector<unsigned char>>& grid, const std::string& title) {
    std::vector<double> x, y, ex, ey;
    for (std::size_t r = 0; r < grid.size(); ++r)
      for (std::size_t j = 0; j < grid[r].size(); ++j) {
        (grid[r][j] ? x : ex).push_back(static_cast<double>(j));
        (grid[r][j] ? y : ey).push_back(static_cast<double>(grid.size() - r));
      }
    auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    plot.points(ex, ey, "", dm::RGB{226, 232, 240}, 3.0).points(x, y, "", dm::RGB{180, 83, 9}, 11.0);
    for (std::size_t j = 0; j < grid.front().size(); ++j)
      plot.segments({static_cast<double>(j)}, {0.5}, {static_cast<double>(j)}, {grid.size() + 0.5}, dm::RGB{148, 163, 184}, 1.0);
    return plot.x_limits(-0.7, grid.front().size() - 0.3).y_limits(0.3, grid.size() + 0.7).title(title).x_label("rod").y_label("row");
  };
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2)
      .add(draw(fall.before, "Before: one row per value"))
      .add(draw(fall.after, "After the beads fall"))
      .size(1100, 470)
      .save_svg("build/bead_small.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Values (tens of minutes): 8 5 7 6 9 6 9 9 5 9 5 8 
Column heights λ′: 12 12 12 12 12 9 7 6 4 
Sorted: 5 5 5 6 6 7 8 8 9 9 9 9 
The fall took 6 ticks and 59 single-cell bead moves; per tick: 23 20 9 4 2 1 .
\image(figures/bead_small.svg)
// result_end

\caption(The first twelve waiting times, in tens of minutes, as rows of beads (top row first) before and after the fall. Each rod keeps its number of beads.)
\alttext(Two dot grids with nine rods and twelve rows. On the left, each row has a ragged number of orange beads from the left edge. On the right, the beads in each rod are stacked at the bottom, so row lengths increase from the top row (5 beads) to the bottom rows (9 beads).)

The twelve values are 8, 5, 7, 6, 9, 6, 9, 9, 5, 9, 5 and 8. Rods 0 to 4 are full, because
every value is at least 5; rod 8 carries the four 9s. After the fall the rows, read from the top,
are 5, 5, 5, 6, 6, 7, 8, 8, 9, 9, 9, 9, the same as `bead_sort` returns. The column heights
\(\lambda'=(12,12,12,12,12,9,7,6,4)\) are unchanged by the fall, as the proof requires.

The simulation moves every bead that has an empty cell below it down one row per tick, and a stack
of beads falls together. It took 6 ticks and 59 single-cell moves, with 23, 20, 9, 4, 2 and 1
moves in successive ticks. Most of the work happens at the start, when every gap begins to close
at once.

> All 272 waiting times

Sorting the full set of waiting times shows a statistical meaning of the column heights. The
fraction of rows longer than \(j\) is \(\lambda'_j/n=\hat S(j)\), the *empirical survival function*:
the proportion of waits longer than \(j\) minutes. The rows of the sorted diagram, read from the
bottom, are the order statistics, the empirical quantile function. Bead sort therefore produces
both, one along each axis of the same diagram.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/bead_faithful.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto faithful = dm::datasets::faithful();
  const auto& waiting = faithful.get_column("waiting").values();
  std::vector<unsigned> minutes(waiting.begin(), waiting.end());
  const double n = static_cast<double>(minutes.size());

  auto sorted = minutes;
  alg::bead_sort(sorted);
  auto reference = minutes;
  std::ranges::sort(reference);
  const auto columns = alg::bead_columns(minutes);
  const auto fall = alg::bead_sort_gravity(minutes);
  std::print("{} waits from {} to {} minutes, {} beads in all. bead_sort matches std::sort: {}.\n", minutes.size(),
             sorted.front(), sorted.back(), std::accumulate(minutes.begin(), minutes.end(), 0ULL),
             sorted == reference ? "yes" : "no");
  std::print("Simulated fall: {} ticks (at most n - 1 = {}), {} single-cell moves.\n\n", fall.ticks, minutes.size() - 1,
             fall.bead_moves);

  std::print("| minutes \\(j\\) | rods taller than … | \\(\\hat S(j)=\\lambda'_j/n\\) |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (const unsigned j : {45U, 55U, 65U, 70U, 75U, 85U, 95U})
    std::print("| {} | {} | {:.3f} |\n", j, columns[j], columns[j] / n);

  // Left: the fallen beads as an image (rows bottom-up, rods across). Right: column heights / n.
  std::vector<double> rod(columns.size()), row(minutes.size());
  for (std::size_t j = 0; j < rod.size(); ++j) rod[j] = static_cast<double>(j);
  for (std::size_t r = 0; r < row.size(); ++r) row[r] = static_cast<double>(r);
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> z(rod.size(), std::vector<double>(row.size()));
  for (std::size_t j = 0; j < rod.size(); ++j)
    for (std::size_t r = 0; r < row.size(); ++r) z[j][r] = columns[j] > r ? 1.0 : 0.0;
  auto image = dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
                   .image(rod, row, z, {dm::RGB{248, 250, 252}, dm::RGB{180, 83, 9}}, 0.0, 1.0)
                   .title("Fallen beads (272 rows)")
                   .x_label("rod j (minutes)")
                   .y_label("row from the bottom");
  std::vector<double> sj, survival;
  for (std::size_t j = 30; j < columns.size(); ++j) {
    sj.push_back(static_cast<double>(j));
    survival.push_back(columns[j] / n);
  }
  auto curve = dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
                   .lines(sj, survival, "", dm::RGB{180, 83, 9}, 2.0)
                   .x_limits(30, 100)
                   .y_limits(0, 1.02)
                   .title("Column heights / n = survival function")
                   .x_label("waiting time j (minutes)")
                   .y_label("fraction of waits longer than j");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2).add(image).add(curve).size(1200, 460).save_svg("build/bead_faithful.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
272 waits from 43 to 96 minutes, 19284 beads in all. bead_sort matches std::sort: yes.
Simulated fall: 245 ticks (at most n - 1 = 271), 280987 single-cell moves.

| minutes \(j\) | rods taller than … | \(\hat S(j)=\lambda'_j/n\) |
| ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 45 | 268 | 0.985 |
| 55 | 213 | 0.783 |
| 65 | 175 | 0.643 |
| 70 | 165 | 0.607 |
| 75 | 138 | 0.507 |
| 85 | 29 | 0.107 |
| 95 | 1 | 0.004 |
\image(figures/bead_faithful.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Left: the 272 waiting times after the beads fall, one row per eruption from the bottom (longest wait) upwards; rod \(j\) is coloured up to its height \(\lambda'_j\). Right: \(\lambda'_j/n\), the fraction of waits longer than \(j\) minutes.)
\alttext(Left: a solid orange staircase on a 96 by 272 grid. The bottom row reaches rod 95 and the top row only rod 42, with the right edge stepping down steeply between rods 75 and 85. Right: a curve that stays at 1 up to about 43 minutes, falls to about 0.6 near 70 minutes, then drops steeply to 0 by 96 minutes, with a shoulder between 60 and 70.)

The 272 waits run from 43 to 96 minutes, so the abacus has 96 rods and 19,284 beads. The result
matches `std::sort` exactly. The table checks the survival-function reading at a few points: 268
of the 272 waits (98.5%) are longer than 45 minutes, and 138 (50.7%) are longer than 75.

The two panels are the same function seen from two sides. Read across, the right-hand edge of the
staircase is the sorted waits. Read up, the height of each rod is \(n\hat S(j)\). The plateau
between about 60 and 70 minutes, where the survival curve is nearly flat, is the gap between Old
Faithful's two modes: few eruptions follow a wait of that length. In the left panel it shows as the
long, nearly horizontal step in the staircase.

Simulating the fall took 245 ticks and 280,987 single-cell moves, about 15 moves per bead. A
bead never falls more than \(n-1\) rows, so \(n-1=271\) ticks is the upper bound for any input
with 272 values.

> What it costs

On a sequential computer, simulating the beads costs at least one step per bead, and there are
\(\sum_ia_i\) beads. The conjugation route costs \(O(n+\max a)\): one pass over the values and two
over a histogram with \(\max a+1\) bins. That is counting sort, whose cost depends on the size of
the values as well as on how many there are. An analogue abacus would take time proportional to
the longest fall, with all beads moving at once. We measure the effect of value size by sorting
the same 272 waits measured in minutes, in seconds, and in milliseconds.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/bead_cost.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

#include <chrono>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto faithful = dm::datasets::faithful();
  const auto& waiting = faithful.get_column("waiting").values();
  const auto time_ms = [](auto&& sort, std::vector<unsigned> v) {
    const auto start = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
    for (int rep = 0; rep < 20; ++rep) {
      auto copy = v;
      sort(copy);
    }
    return std::chrono::duration<double, std::milli>(std::chrono::steady_clock::now() - start).count() / 20.0;
  };
  std::print("| unit | largest value | beads \\(\\sum a_i\\) | bead_sort (ms) | std::sort (ms) |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::vector<double> log_max, log_bead, log_std;
  for (const auto& [unit, scale] : {std::pair{"minutes", 1.0}, {"seconds", 60.0}, {"milliseconds", 60000.0}}) {
    std::vector<unsigned> v;
    for (const double w : waiting) v.push_back(static_cast<unsigned>(w * scale));
    const double bead = time_ms([](auto& x) { alg::bead_sort(x); }, v);
    const double std_sort = time_ms([](auto& x) { std::ranges::sort(x); }, v);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} |\n", unit, std::ranges::max(v),
               std::accumulate(v.begin(), v.end(), 0ULL), bead, std_sort);
  }
  for (double e = 1.5; e <= 7.01; e += 0.5) {
    const double scale = std::pow(10.0, e) / 96.0;
    std::vector<unsigned> v;
    for (const double w : waiting) v.push_back(static_cast<unsigned>(w * scale));
    log_max.push_back(e);
    log_bead.push_back(std::log10(time_ms([](auto& x) { alg::bead_sort(x); }, v)));
    log_std.push_back(std::log10(time_ms([](auto& x) { std::ranges::sort(x); }, v)));
  }
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .lines(log_max, log_bead, "bead_sort", dm::RGB{180, 83, 9}, 2.0)
      .points(log_max, log_bead, "", dm::RGB{180, 83, 9}, 6.0)
      .lines(log_max, log_std, "std::sort", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 2.0)
      .points(log_max, log_std, "", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 6.0)
      .y_limits(-3.6, 2.4)
      .title("Sorting 272 values: time against value size")
      .x_label("log10 of the largest value")
      .y_label("log10 time (ms)")
      .size(820, 440)
      .save_svg("build/bead_cost.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| unit | largest value | beads \(\sum a_i\) | bead_sort (ms) | std::sort (ms) |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| minutes | 96 | 19284 | 0.0009 | 0.0014 |
| seconds | 5760 | 1157040 | 0.0059 | 0.0007 |
| milliseconds | 5760000 | 1157040000 | 16.8041 | 0.0014 |
\image(figures/bead_cost.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Time to sort the 272 waiting times, rescaled so that the largest value is between \(10^{1.5}\) and \(10^7\), by bead sort and by `std::sort`, on log scales.)
\alttext(Log-log plot. The std::sort line stays flat near 10 to the minus 3.2 milliseconds for all value sizes. The bead_sort line starts slightly below it at small values, crosses it near a largest value of 100, and then rises with slope close to one, reaching about 25 milliseconds when the largest value is 10 million.)

With the waits in minutes, `bead_sort` takes about 0.7 µs on this machine and beats
`std::sort`, at about 1 µs. The histogram has only 97 bins and every pass is short and
predictable. Measured in seconds, the largest value is 5,760 and `bead_sort` already takes nine
times as long as `std::sort`. In milliseconds it needs a histogram of 5.76 million bins, and takes
13 ms against 1 µs, about 13,000 times slower. The values and their order are the same in all
three cases; only the units differ.

Above a largest value of about \(10^3\), the bead_sort curve has slope close to 1 on the log-log
plot, so its time grows in proportion to \(\max a\), as the \(O(n+\max a)\) bound says. `std::sort`
is flat, because it only compares values and their size does not matter. The bead simulation
itself would be worse again, since it moves at least one cell per bead: in milliseconds there
would be over \(10^9\) beads.

> Summary

- Gravity on an abacus conjugates a Ferrers diagram, and reading the rows conjugates it again,
  so the rows come out sorted. This proves that bead sort is correct.
- The two conjugations need only a histogram and suffix sums, so `algorithms::bead_sort` runs
  in \(O(n+\max a)\) without moving any beads. `algorithms::bead_columns` returns the column
  heights, and `algorithms::bead_sort_gravity` simulates the fall.
- For data, the column heights are \(n\) times the empirical survival function and the rows are
  the order statistics. Old Faithful's gap between 60 and 70 minutes appears as a flat stretch of
  the survival curve.
- The cost depends on the size of the values. For the waits in minutes bead sort is slightly faster
  than `std::sort`; the same waits in milliseconds take about 13,000 times as long. Bead sort is a
  form of counting sort and suits only small non-negative integers.

> References

- Arulanandham, J. J., Calude, C. S. and Dinneen, M. J. (2002). Bead-sort: a natural sorting
  algorithm. *Bulletin of the EATCS* 76, 153–162.
- Andrews, G. E. (1976). *The Theory of Partitions*. Addison-Wesley. Chapter 1 (Ferrers
  diagrams and conjugation).
- Azzalini, A. and Bowman, A. W. (1990). A look at some data on the Old Faithful geyser.
  *Applied Statistics* 39, 357–365.
